# Everything the build does not need, kept out of the context.
#
# Two reasons beyond speed. node_modules and bin/obj are built inside the image from the manifests, and
# copying a host's versions in would mean the image depends on whatever the machine that built it
# happened to have lying about. And .env holds real secrets: it is gitignored, which stops it reaching
# the repository and does nothing at all to stop it reaching an image layer.

.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Dumps are whole copies of the database. Into an image layer is the last place they belong.
backups/
*.dump

**/node_modules/
**/bin/
**/obj/
**/dist/
**/.vs/
**/.idea/
.git/
.github/
.claude/
.pnpm-store/
.tmp-test-bin/

# The test project is not published into the image; only its .csproj is copied, for the restore.
Tests/**/bin/
Tests/**/obj/

*.md
!README.md
docker-compose*.yml
Dockerfile
start-dev.bat
